{"chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_000", "paper_id": "2608.28678v1", "title": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "authors": ["Matthew Perlman", "James Beetham", "Niels Da Vitoria Lobo", "Amrit Singh Bedi", "Mubarak Shah"], "year": 2026, "venue": "SIGGRAPH / ACM TOG (arXiv cs.GR)", "section": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "subsection": null, "page": 1, "previous_page_number": null, "next_page_number": null, "chunk_index": 0, "element_type": "text", "parent_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_section_evaluating_constrained_iterative_refinement_for_scalable_vector_graphics_generation_with_off_the_shelf_vlms", "previous_chunk_id": null, "next_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_001", "text": "Matthew Perlman"}
{"chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_001", "paper_id": "2608.28678v1", "title": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "authors": ["Matthew Perlman", "James Beetham", "Niels Da Vitoria Lobo", "Amrit Singh Bedi", "Mubarak Shah"], "year": 2026, "venue": "SIGGRAPH / ACM TOG (arXiv cs.GR)", "section": "Abstract", "subsection": null, "page": 1, "previous_page_number": null, "next_page_number": null, "chunk_index": 1, "element_type": "text", "parent_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_section_abstract", "previous_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_000", "next_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_002", "text": "Scalable Vector Graphics (SVGs) power much of the modern visual ecosystem, yet state-of-the-art generative models focus almost entirely on rasterized images. We explore whether inference-time methods can unlock SVG generation capabilities in offthe-shelf vision-language models (VLMs). We systematically evaluate a constrained iterative refinement harness that combines visual feedback, structured editing, and constrained decoding to characterize the capabilities and limitations of current VLMs for SVG generation. Across multiple VLMs and generation settings, we find that constrained decoding improves compilation success rates, while iterative refinement reveals a deficit in visual reasoning and self-correction. Our results highlight both the promise and current limitations of using inference-time methods to adapt general-purpose VLMs for SVG generation."}
{"chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_002", "paper_id": "2608.28678v1", "title": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "authors": ["Matthew Perlman", "James Beetham", "Niels Da Vitoria Lobo", "Amrit Singh Bedi", "Mubarak Shah"], "year": 2026, "venue": "SIGGRAPH / ACM TOG (arXiv cs.GR)", "section": "CCS Concepts", "subsection": null, "page": 1, "previous_page_number": null, "next_page_number": null, "chunk_index": 2, "element_type": "text", "parent_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_section_ccs_concepts", "previous_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_001", "next_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_003", "text": "- Computing methodologies → Computer graphics; Artificial intelligence;"}
{"chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_003", "paper_id": "2608.28678v1", "title": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "authors": ["Matthew Perlman", "James Beetham", "Niels Da Vitoria Lobo", "Amrit Singh Bedi", "Mubarak Shah"], "year": 2026, "venue": "SIGGRAPH / ACM TOG (arXiv cs.GR)", "section": "1. Introduction", "subsection": null, "page": 1, "previous_page_number": null, "next_page_number": null, "chunk_index": 3, "element_type": "text", "parent_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_section_1_introduction", "previous_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_002", "next_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_004", "text": "SVGs are widely used for web, interface, and illustration content because they are resolution-independent, compact, and directly editable. Despite the practical benefits of SVGs, generating them remains challenging for general-purpose VLMs: the model must jointly produce valid code, reason about spatial relationships, and translate precise visual details into a new medium. Existing SVG generation methods [YCC ∗ 25] address these challenges through specialized architectures or task-specific training, decoupling them from the rapidly improving frontier multimodal models.\nWe investigate whether inference-time methods are sufficient to make off-the-shelf VLMs effective SVG generators. We introduce a lightweight harness: an external framework that wraps a VLM with rendering, visual feedback, structured editing, and constrained generation. Using this harness, we evaluate iterative refinement, four levels of editing structure, and grammar-constrained decoding [GJPW23] for text-to-SVG and image-to-SVG generation across multiple model scales. Our study characterizes when these mechanisms improve syntactic reliability, affect visual quality, and how effectively VLMs translate rendered feedback into code changes."}
{"chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_004", "paper_id": "2608.28678v1", "title": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "authors": ["Matthew Perlman", "James Beetham", "Niels Da Vitoria Lobo", "Amrit Singh Bedi", "Mubarak Shah"], "year": 2026, "venue": "SIGGRAPH / ACM TOG (arXiv cs.GR)", "section": "2. System Overview", "subsection": null, "page": 1, "previous_page_number": null, "next_page_number": null, "chunk_index": 4, "element_type": "text", "parent_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_section_2_system_overview", "previous_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_003", "next_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_005", "text": "Our harness, shown in Figure 1, combines constrained decoding of VLM outputs with iterative refinement using visual feedback, enabling reliable structured generation and visual self-correction.\n© 2026 The Author(s).\nProceedings published by Eurographics - The European Association for Computer Graphics. This is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.\nFigure 1: Our proposed harness. Given a user query, the VLM generates a constrained output specifying an SVG edit. We parse the action, update and render the SVG, and return the code and image to the VLM for the next refinement iteration ."}
{"chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_005", "paper_id": "2608.28678v1", "title": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "authors": ["Matthew Perlman", "James Beetham", "Niels Da Vitoria Lobo", "Amrit Singh Bedi", "Mubarak Shah"], "year": 2026, "venue": "SIGGRAPH / ACM TOG (arXiv cs.GR)", "section": "2.1. Constrained Decoding", "subsection": null, "page": 1, "previous_page_number": null, "next_page_number": 2, "chunk_index": 5, "element_type": "text", "parent_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_section_2_1_constrained_decoding", "previous_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_004", "next_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_006", "text": "Many smaller models struggle to produce correct JSON structure and SVG code, motivating inference-time constraints. For closedsource models (GPT-5.6), we enforce JSON schemas to ensure valid property names and structure; for open-source models, we use a context-free grammar (CFG) over a tractable subset of SVG elements (e.g., rect , circle , path) and their attributes, producing syntactically valid SVGs by construction. Grammar-constrained decoding is not exposed by most closed-source models.\n1\n, James Beetham 2\n, Niels Da Vitoria Lobo 2\n, Amrit Singh Bedi 2\n, Mubarak Shah 2\n1 University of Massachusetts Amherst 2 University of Central Florida"}
