{"chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_011", "paper_id": "2608.28678v1", "title": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "authors": ["Matthew Perlman", "James Beetham", "Niels Da Vitoria Lobo", "Amrit Singh Bedi", "Mubarak Shah"], "year": 2026, "venue": "SIGGRAPH / ACM TOG (arXiv cs.GR)", "section": "4. Discussion", "subsection": null, "page": 2, "previous_page_number": null, "next_page_number": null, "chunk_index": 11, "element_type": "text", "parent_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_section_4_discussion", "previous_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_010", "next_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_012", "text": "Our results suggest that reliable SVG generation requires more than syntactic constraints or repeated visual feedback. Constrained decoding can improve validity while reducing visual quality, while iterative refinement exposes a limitation in current VLMs' ability to translate rendered errors into precise vector-level corrections. These findings highlight visual quality and structural validity as distinct objectives that must be jointly addressed.\nIn the future, richer action spaces, alternative visual feedback mechanisms, stronger constrained decoding strategies, and prompt meta-optimization all have the possibility to improve generation quality and success rate. Beyond extending our harness, a deeper mechanistic analysis of the model's failure to incorporate visual feedback may inform improvements to general-purpose VLM spatial-reasoning capabilities."}
{"chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_012", "paper_id": "2608.28678v1", "title": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "authors": ["Matthew Perlman", "James Beetham", "Niels Da Vitoria Lobo", "Amrit Singh Bedi", "Mubarak Shah"], "year": 2026, "venue": "SIGGRAPH / ACM TOG (arXiv cs.GR)", "section": "References", "subsection": null, "page": 2, "previous_page_number": null, "next_page_number": null, "chunk_index": 12, "element_type": "text", "parent_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_section_references", "previous_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_011", "next_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_013", "text": "[GJPW23] GENG S., JOSIFOSKI M., PEYRARD M., WEST R.: Grammar-constrained decoding for structured NLP tasks without finetuning. In EMNLP (2023), pp. 10932–10952. 1\n[Ope26] OPENAI: Gpt-5.6. OpenAI API, 2026. Accessed: 202607-13. URL: https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/ models . 2\n[Qwe26] QWEN TEAM: Qwen3.5. https://huggingface.co/ Qwen, 2026. Accessed: 2026-07-13. 2\n[YCC ∗ 25] YANG Y., CHENG W., CHEN S., ZENG X., YIN F., ZHANG J., WANG L., YU G., MA X., JIANG Y.-G.: Omnisvg: A unified scalable vector graphics generation model. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS) (2025). 1 , 2"}
{"chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_013", "paper_id": "2608.28678v1", "title": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "authors": ["Matthew Perlman", "James Beetham", "Niels Da Vitoria Lobo", "Amrit Singh Bedi", "Mubarak Shah"], "year": 2026, "venue": "SIGGRAPH / ACM TOG (arXiv cs.GR)", "section": "2.2. Iterative Refinement", "subsection": null, "page": 2, "previous_page_number": null, "next_page_number": null, "chunk_index": 13, "element_type": "text", "parent_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_section_2_2_iterative_refinement", "previous_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_012", "next_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_014", "text": "We explore three refinement variants which aim to improve the generated SVG at each iteration, along with a single-turn baseline:\nSingle Turn. A baseline where the model generates the full SVG once, with no iterative refinement or visual feedback.\nUnstructured Refinement. The model regenerates the entire SVG at each iteration, rebuilding the image from scratch.\nSemi-Structured Refinement. The model selects an action and generates an SVG code snippet used to add or replace an element.\nStructured Refinement. The model outputs an action and element parameters (e.g. a rectangle's position, width, height, and color) which the harness converts into a full SVG snippet.\nAll variants include free-form reasoning before the generated code or action in JSON. Semi-Structured and Structured use the actions Add , Update , Remove, and Exit, with a target element ID when required. Unstructured refinement is capped at 10 steps, while the more granular Semi-Structured and Structured allow up to 20."}
{"chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_014", "paper_id": "2608.28678v1", "title": "Evaluating Constrained Iterative Refinement for Scalable Vector Graphics Generation with Off-the-Shelf VLMs", "authors": ["Matthew Perlman", "James Beetham", "Niels Da Vitoria Lobo", "Amrit Singh Bedi", "Mubarak Shah"], "year": 2026, "venue": "SIGGRAPH / ACM TOG (arXiv cs.GR)", "section": "3. Results", "subsection": null, "page": 2, "previous_page_number": null, "next_page_number": null, "chunk_index": 14, "element_type": "text", "parent_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_section_3_results", "previous_chunk_id": "2608.28678v1_chunk_013", "next_chunk_id": null, "text": "We evaluate our framework on MMSVG-Bench [YCC ∗ 25] across multiple model scales (Qwen3.5 9B and 27B) [Qwe26], with and without grammar-constrained decoding, and across all four generation pipelines (Table 1). Metrics are computed over successfully compiled images. We provide example images generated by GPT5.6 [Ope26] using each pipeline and decoding strategy in Figure 2 .\nConstrained Decoding Hurts Visual Quality. Across visual metrics and generation settings, constrained decoding generally resulted in lower quality images. We hypothesize that enforcing our limited grammar steers token generation into unlikely and visually flawed SVG elements.\nConstrained Decoding Can Improve Compilation. We find that constrained decoding substantially improves compilation rates for most pipelines, a result consistent with our initial hypothesis.\nSmall Models Struggle With Visual Feedback. We find that for the models tested, iterative refinement has an inconsistent effect on visual quality, and often lowers compilation rates by introducing more opportunities to break. Our testing suggests small models struggle to link visual inconsistencies to the underlying SVG code."}
